# Move the drone's own camera in the body frame.
#
# `pitch` is the one that matters and the one that has cost the most time: at -28.6 deg -
# which is **-0.5**, the number you actually pass - level flight sits in the top 13.7% of the
# frame, so a backend that does not know the goal ALTITUDE will fly itself into the ground.
# Changing it invalidates comparisons against every episode scored at the old value - it is a
# measurement surface, not a preference.
#
# The two numbers are spelled out together deliberately. This header quoting "-28.6 deg"
# directly above a field marked "degrees" is what made the misreading below so easy.
float64[3] xyz                  # metres, body FRD, from the vehicle origin
float64 roll                    # RADIANS
float64 pitch                   # RADIANS - this file claimed otherwise, and was wrong.
float64 yaw                     # RADIANS
#
# The values go unchanged to `airsim.to_quaternion(pitch, roll, yaw)`
# (sim_bridge/carla_air/camera.py:50), which is built from `math.cos(yaw * 0.5)` and friends -
# radians. `run_episode.py`'s `--camera-pitch` is radians too and says so. Only this comment
# disagreed, and it is the only documentation a client reading the interface ever sees.
#
# CONFIRMED IN FLIGHT 2026-08-29, through this service, rather than by reading the code:
#
#     pitch field = -0.5   -> camera measured at -28.6 deg on /camera/pose   (correct)
#     pitch field = -28.6  -> a rotation of about 161 deg
#
# and the frames say the rest. At -0.5 the camera sees the city - beach, buildings, horizon.
# At -28.6 it sees the aircraft's own rotors and fuselage filling the frame, from in front at
# 0.8 m. That is what a client got for believing the word "degrees": every frame the model saw
# was airframe, and three separate conclusions were drawn from that view before anyone opened
# a frame and looked at it.
---
bool success
string message
